I used Prim's algorithm with a min heap. Start from node 0, always take the lightest edge that reaches an unvisited node, and add its weight to the answer. Each time a node is added, its outgoing edges are pushed into the heap.

If the graph is disconnected, the heap becomes empty before all vertices are used, so the code returns `-1`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int spanningTree(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[]) {
        if (V == 0) return 0;

        vector<bool> used(V, false);
        priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> minHeap;
        minHeap.push({0, 0});

        int totalWeight = 0;
        int taken = 0;

        while (!minHeap.empty() && taken < V) {
            auto [weight, node] = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            if (used[node]) continue;

            used[node] = true;
            taken++;
            totalWeight += weight;

            for (const auto& edge : adj[node]) {
                int nextNode = edge[0];
                int nextWeight = edge[1];
                if (!used[nextNode]) {
                    minHeap.push({nextWeight, nextNode});
                }
            }
        }

        return taken == V ? totalWeight : -1;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(E log E)` in the usual heap implementation, and space complexity: `O(V + E)`. The input adjacency list is only read, not changed.

A second Prim implementation can avoid sorting every edge first. The priority queue keeps possible connections ordered by weight. Stale entries are harmless because a vertex is checked with `visited` when it comes out.

This also handles parallel edges naturally. If two edges point to the same neighbor, the lighter one will normally be picked first.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int spanningTree(int V, List<List<List<Integer>>> adj) {
        if (V == 0) return 0;

        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];
        PriorityQueue<int[]> queue = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(edge -> edge[0]));
        queue.offer(new int[]{0, 0});

        int totalWeight = 0;
        int count = 0;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] current = queue.poll();
            int weight = current[0];
            int node = current[1];

            if (visited[node]) continue;

            visited[node] = true;
            count++;
            totalWeight += weight;

            for (List<Integer> edge : adj.get(node)) {
                int neighbor = edge.get(0);
                int nextWeight = edge.get(1);
                if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                    queue.offer(new int[]{nextWeight, neighbor});
                }
            }
        }

        return count == V ? totalWeight : -1;
    }
}
```

Dry run: the queue begins with `(0, 0)`. After node `0` is accepted, all edges from it are added. The smallest of those edges selects the next node, and its edges are added too. The total is the sum of accepted edge weights.

The algorithm takes `O(E log E)` time and `O(V + E)` extra space. It only reads the platform's adjacency list.

Kruskal's method feels convenient here because the graph is already given as an adjacency list. I turn each undirected pair into one edge, sort all edges by weight, and use a disjoint set to avoid cycles.

The `parent` and `rank` arrays keep track of which vertices are already connected. When an edge joins two different groups, it belongs to the spanning tree.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private int find(int node, int[] parent) {
        if (parent[node] == node) {
            return node;
        }
        parent[node] = find(parent[node], parent);
        return parent[node];
    }

    public int spanningTree(int V, List<List<List<Integer>>> adj) {
        List<int[]> edges = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {
            for (List<Integer> edge : adj.get(u)) {
                int v = edge.get(0);
                int weight = edge.get(1);
                if (u < v) {
                    edges.add(new int[]{weight, u, v});
                }
            }
        }

        edges.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(edge -> edge[0]));

        int[] parent = new int[V];
        int[] rank = new int[V];
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;
        }

        int totalWeight = 0;
        int selected = 0;

        for (int[] edge : edges) {
            int weight = edge[0];
            int first = find(edge[1], parent);
            int second = find(edge[2], parent);

            if (first == second) continue;

            if (rank[first] < rank[second]) {
                parent[first] = second;
            } else if (rank[first] > rank[second]) {
                parent[second] = first;
            } else {
                parent[second] = first;
                rank[first]++;
            }

            totalWeight += weight;
            selected++;
            if (selected == V - 1) break;
        }

        return selected == V - 1 ? totalWeight : -1;
    }
}
```

For example, if the sorted edges have weights `1, 2, 2`, the first two that connect separate groups are selected. A later edge inside the same group is skipped because it would make a cycle.

The time cost is `O(E log E)`, mostly for sorting. The extra space is `O(V + E)`. The code does not modify `adj`.

This is the basic Prim version without a heap. `best[node]` stores the cheapest edge currently able to connect that node to the growing tree. On every round, scan all vertices to find the unused one with the smallest value.

It is not the fastest choice for a large sparse graph, but the steps are easy to follow and the code works directly with the supplied adjacency list.

```python
class Solution:
    def spanningTree(self, V, adj):
        if V == 0:
            return 0

        infinity = 10**18
        best = [infinity] * V
        used = [False] * V
        best[0] = 0
        total_weight = 0

        for _ in range(V):
            node = -1
            for i in range(V):
                if not used[i] and (node == -1 or best[i] < best[node]):
                    node = i

            if node == -1 or best[node] == infinity:
                return -1

            used[node] = True
            total_weight += best[node]

            for neighbor, weight in adj[node]:
                if not used[neighbor] and weight < best[neighbor]:
                    best[neighbor] = weight

        return total_weight
```

The starting vertex contributes `0`, and every later vertex contributes the edge that connects it to the tree. For a single vertex, the loop adds zero, which is the expected result.

Time complexity: `O(V^2 + E)`. Space complexity: `O(V)`, apart from the input graph. The adjacency list stays unchanged.

Here the graph is converted into a normal edge list and processed from lightest to heaviest. A disjoint set tells whether two endpoints are already connected, so an edge is added only when it joins two separate components.

The `u < v` check avoids adding both copies of an undirected adjacency entry. If fewer than `V - 1` edges are accepted, the graph is not connected and no spanning tree exists.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int findParent(int node, vector<int>& parent) {
        if (parent[node] == node) {
            return node;
        }
        return parent[node] = findParent(parent[node], parent);
    }

    int spanningTree(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[]) {
        vector<array<int, 3>> edges;

        for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {
            for (const auto& edge : adj[u]) {
                int v = edge[0];
                int weight = edge[1];
                if (u < v) {
                    edges.push_back({weight, u, v});
                }
            }
        }

        sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());

        vector<int> parent(V), size(V, 1);
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);

        int totalWeight = 0;
        int chosen = 0;

        for (const auto& edge : edges) {
            int weight = edge[0];
            int u = edge[1];
            int v = edge[2];

            int rootU = findParent(u, parent);
            int rootV = findParent(v, parent);
            if (rootU == rootV) continue;

            if (size[rootU] < size[rootV]) {
                swap(rootU, rootV);
            }
            parent[rootV] = rootU;
            size[rootU] += size[rootV];

            totalWeight += weight;
            chosen++;
            if (chosen == V - 1) break;
        }

        return chosen == V - 1 ? totalWeight : -1;
    }
};
```

One edge case is `V == 1`: no edge is needed, so `chosen` is already equal to `V - 1` and the function returns `0`. Self-loops are ignored by the `u < v` condition.

Sorting takes `O(E log E)` time, while the disjoint set operations are nearly constant each. Overall space usage is `O(V + E)`. The input array is not modified.

