## Intuition
Prim’s algorithm grows a Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) by always choosing the cheapest edge that connects any visited vertex to an unvisited vertex. You can visualize it like expanding an “island” of visited nodes: at each step, build the cheapest bridge to bring in one new node.
Two pragmatic insights make it interview‑friendly and fast:

Use a min‑heap keyed by edge weight so the next bridge is always the cheapest.
Early exit once you’ve added all V vertices to the MST (equivalently, once you’ve added V‑1 edges). This avoids popping extra stale heap entries that were pushed earlier and can save time on dense graphs.

## Approach
Min‑heap (priority queue): Store pairs {weight, node}. We may push duplicate candidates; we simply skip when a popped node is already visited (no decrease‑key needed).
Visited array: vis[u] marks whether u is already in the MST.
Seed: Push {0, 0} to start from node 0 with zero cost.
Main loop:

Pop the smallest {wt, u}. If vis[u] is set, skip (stale).
Otherwise, mark u, add wt to sum, and increment a picked counter.
Early exit: if picked == V, break—the MST is complete.
For each neighbor (v, w) of u, if v is unvisited, push {w, v} into the heap.


Return the total weight sum.

This pattern yields the classic O(E log V) runtime with adjacency lists and a binary heap, and O(V + E) auxiliary space for the visited array and heap.

### 🔎 Why early exit helps
Without it, the loop ends only when the heap is empty—yet the heap often contains extra stale entries from previous relaxations. Breaking as soon as the MST is complete avoids popping them, which can shave time on dense graphs and large test cases.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
public:
    int spanningTree(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[]) {
        // Prim's Algorithm with early exit
        // Time: O(E log V), Space: O(V + E)

        using PII = pair<int,int>; // {weight, node}
        priority_queue<PII, vector<PII>, greater<PII>> pq;

        vector<char> vis(V, 0);
        long long sum = 0;

        // Start from node 0 with 0 cost edge
        pq.push({0, 0});

        int picked = 0; // number of vertices added to MST

        while (!pq.empty()) {
            auto [wt, node] = pq.top();
            pq.pop();

            if (vis[node]) continue;   // stale entry (we already included this node)
            vis[node] = 1;
            sum += wt;
            picked++;

            // ✅ Early exit: once all vertices are in MST, we are done
            if (picked == V) break;

            // Explore neighbors
            for (auto &nbr : adj[node]) {   // nbr = {adjNode, edgeWt}
                int adjNode = nbr[0];
                int edgeWt  = nbr[1];
                if (!vis[adjNode]) {
                    pq.push({edgeWt, adjNode});
                }
            }
        }

        return static_cast<int>(sum); // per GFG function spec
    }
};

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
Time Complexity: O(E log V). Each edge can lead to at most one heap push per endpoint considered, and heap operations are log V. Early exit stops the loop immediately after all V vertices are included, avoiding unnecessary pops of stale entries.


**Space Complexity:**
Space Complexity: O(V + E). The visited array costs O(V); the priority queue can hold up to O(E) candidate edges in the worst case. The graph itself is provided as an adjacency list (O(V + E)).litora.
